    <h2>What makes Level 5 tricky<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>All eleven cards end at the girl. There is no second target, no container to tap open, no order to respect — one drop zone, eleven sources, which makes the level look easier than it is. The work is finding the sources, and they grow less plausible as the list runs: a bat on the ground (1), a doll (2), the track in the bottom right corner (3), the phone stand in front of the statue (4), the unicycle inside the billboard (5), then the billboard itself (6).</p>
    <p>Size is not a filter. A ride track and a whole billboard travel to the girl, and both are painted as fairground scenery rather than as props, so nothing about them suggests they can be picked up until you try. The billboard is a container and an item on consecutive cards: the unicycle comes out of it (5), then the board goes to the girl (6). The trash can repeats that idea with a gesture instead of a sequence — swipe up (9) and the iron rod inside becomes draggable (10). If the rod will not move, the can has not been swiped yet.</p>
    <p>Cards 7 and 8 complete the scenery trap. The vine growing outside the box and the chain hanging on the pillar are plain drags, but both read as set dressing the same way the track does — nothing highlights them, and neither sits inside a container to open first. Card 9 is the only non-drag in the level: the trash can wants a swipe up, not a drag, which breaks the rhythm of ten straight carries. That single gesture switch is the level's real test — ten drags and one swipe, and the board only takes them in that order.</p>
    <p>The last card is the one runs leave behind. Step 11 drags the headphones from the girl's head to the girl — the same person on both ends, which looks like a mistake and is the input that closes the level. Everything before it has been carried to her from somewhere else on the fairground; the final item she is already wearing. If the scene looks settled and the polaroid has not appeared, that is the card to replay. The FAQ repeats the eleven in a single paragraph if the list blurred.</p>

    <h2>Two chains and a self-drag<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Only two cards depend on another card, and both hide it well:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The unicycle (5) comes out of the billboard before the billboard itself goes to the girl (6).</li>
      <li>Swipe up on the trash can (9), then the iron rod inside becomes draggable (10).</li>
      <li>The track (3) and the phone stand (4) are scenery, not props.</li>
      <li>Card 11 takes the headphones off the girl and hands them back to her.</li>
      <li>Every card in the level ends at the same target: the girl.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>One target for eleven cards is the whole design, and the last card breaks only the logic of where items come from.</p>

    <h2>FAQ<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
   
    <details><summary>What is the answer to Brain Puzzle 3 Level 5?</summary><p>In order: 1) Drag the baseball bat on the ground to the girl; 2) Drag the doll to the girl; 3) Drag the track in the bottom right corner to the girl; 4) Drag the phone stand in front of the statue to the girl; 5) Drag the unicycle inside the billboard to the girl; 6) Drag the billboard to the girl; 7) Drag the vine outside the box to the girl; 8) Drag the chain on the pillar to the girl; 9) Swipe up on the trash can; 10) Drag the iron rod inside to the girl; 11) Drag the headphones from the girl's head to her</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does nothing happen when I tap in Level 5?</summary><p>Level 5 has no taps at all — the trash can needs an upward swipe at step 9 and the other ten cards are drags onto the girl, so pressing a prop does nothing.</p></details>
